<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<div class="callout"><h2 id="glance" style="margin-top:0">Dead hang variations at a glance</h2>
        <ol class="stop-steps">
          <li><strong>Start here:</strong> the overhand passive hang is the starting point. Keep double overhand as the main hang.</li>
          <li><strong>Change the grip:</strong> underhand, neutral and mixed grips change wrist and shoulder position for variety.</li>
          <li><strong>Change the shoulder:</strong> an active hang adds shoulder blade control and is usually harder to hold than a passive hang.</li>
          <li><strong>Make it harder:</strong> towel, L-hang, weighted and one-arm hangs. Weighted needs about 45 seconds first and one-arm about 60 seconds.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The sets and holds on this page are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p></div>

        <h2>Which dead hang variation should you use?</h2>
        <p>Use the variation that matches your goal. The overhand passive hang is the starting point, an active hang adds shoulder blade control, and towel, L-hang, weighted and one-arm hangs make the hold harder. The table compares all ten by what they change, how hard they are on this site and who they suit.</p>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Dead hang variations compared" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Variation</th><th scope="col">What it changes or trains</th><th scope="col">Level on this site</th><th scope="col">Who it suits</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Passive</strong></td><td>Relaxed shoulders hang from the bar</td><td>Starting point</td><td>Stretching after sitting and grip endurance</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Active</strong></td><td>Shoulder blades pulled down with straight arms</td><td>Usually harder to hold than passive</td><td>Pull-up preparation</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Overhand</strong></td><td>Default grip with palms facing away</td><td>Starting point</td><td>General grip strength training</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Underhand</strong></td><td>Wrist and shoulder position, with the biceps tendon under stretch</td><td>Optional swap</td><td>Variety, and some people find it easier on the wrists</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Neutral</strong></td><td>Natural wrist position on parallel handles</td><td>Optional swap</td><td>People who find overhand uncomfortable</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Mixed grip</strong></td><td>Uneven hands that can make the trunk work to stay straight</td><td>Optional extra</td><td>Variety, and lifters who deadlift with a mixed grip</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Towel</strong></td><td>Support grip on a thicker, softer surface</td><td>Harder than a bar hold</td><td>Climbers and anyone who wants variety</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>L-hang</strong></td><td>Core and hip flexors hold the legs at 90 degrees</td><td>Harder, because the abdominals also tire</td><td>Core training</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Weighted</strong></td><td>External load on the same hold</td><td>Advanced: after about 45 seconds comfortable</td><td>People whose bodyweight hang is easy</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>One-arm</strong></td><td>Bodyweight through one forearm, shoulder and side of the trunk</td><td>Advanced: after about 60 seconds two-arm</td><td>Experienced hangers and climbers</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h2>Why Vary Your Dead Hangs?</h2>
        <p>Varying your dead hangs changes grip, shoulder position and load. Progress can slow when you repeat the same hang for many weeks. A variation gives you a new task.</p>
        <p>Overhand, underhand and neutral grips change wrist and shoulder position. Your finger flexors do the holding in all three. Towel hangs and weighted hangs make the hold harder in other ways. Each variation fills a gap the others leave.</p>
        <p>Shoulder position changes the training effect. A passive hang stretches the shoulders and trunk. An active hang trains shoulder blade control. Both use the same bar and the same body position. The only difference is shoulder blade engagement.</p>
        <p>Choose variations based on your goal. The guide below covers each option with technique cues and recommended programming. The sets and holds on this page are common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>

        <h2>Passive Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>The passive dead hang is the standard starting point. Grab the bar with an overhand grip and let your shoulders relax completely. Your upper traps lengthen under your bodyweight and your shoulder opens toward its full overhead range.</p>
        <p>Hanging unloads the spine for as long as you hang. How much this helps back stiffness has not been established.</p>
        <p>Use passive hangs for shoulder stretching and grip endurance. Many people use passive hangs as a stretch after sitting. Hold 15 to 60 seconds for 3 to 4 sets. Read the full comparison in the <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive vs active hang guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>Active Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>The active dead hang adds shoulder blade depression to the standard hold. Pull your shoulder blades down and together while keeping your arms straight. Your shoulders drop a few centimeters from the passive position.</p>
        <p>Your lats and lower traps work to hold the shoulder blades in place. This practices the shoulder blade position used at the start of a pull-up. Scapular control is one of several things that matter for a first pull-up.</p>
        <p>Hold active hangs for 15 to 45 seconds across 3 to 4 sets. Active hangs are usually harder to hold than passive hangs. Alternate active and passive hangs across sessions.</p>

        <h2>Overhand (Pronated) Grip</h2>
        <p>Palms face away from you with the backs of your hands facing up. This is the default dead hang grip. Overhand, underhand and neutral grips change wrist and shoulder position. Your finger flexors do the holding in all three.</p>
        <p>Pull-ups, rows and deadlifts all use an overhand grip as the standard pulling position. An overhand hang matches the grip you use in them.</p>
        <p>Use overhand grip for general <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength training</a>. Most dead hang programming defaults to this grip. Hold 15 to 60 seconds for 3 to 4 sets.</p>

        <h2>Underhand (Supinated) Grip</h2>
        <p>Palms face toward you with the insides of your forearms facing up. An underhand grip puts the biceps tendon under stretch. Stop if the front of the elbow hurts.</p>
        <p>Some people find underhand hangs more comfortable on the wrists. Try it and stop if it hurts. The shoulder reaches a slightly different overhead position.</p>
        <p>Use underhand grip for variety when your forearms need a change. Hold 20 to 60 seconds for 3 sets. Alternate with overhand across sessions.</p>

        <h2>Neutral Grip</h2>
        <p>Palms face each other on parallel handles or a neutral-grip pull-up bar. Many people find a neutral grip the most comfortable. The grip allows a natural wrist position with no forced rotation.</p>
        <p>Use neutral grip for variety and comfort. It suits people who find overhand uncomfortable. Hold 15 to 60 seconds for 3 sets.</p>
        <p>Ask a clinician before hanging if you have shoulder or wrist pain. The <a href="/shoulder-health/">shoulder health guide</a> covers what is known about hanging with shoulder problems.</p>

        <h2>Mixed Grip Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>One hand grips overhand and the other grips underhand. A mixed grip can make the body want to turn. You may feel the trunk working to stay still.</p>
        <p>Mixed grip hangs are optional. The main hang training should stay double overhand. Keep the underhand elbow straight and stop if the front of the elbow hurts.</p>
        <p>Alternate which hand goes over and under between sets. Hold 20 to 40 seconds for 3 sets and keep the volume equal on each side. Read the full <a href="/mixed-grip-hang/">mixed grip dead hang guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>Towel Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>Drape a towel over the bar and grip the towel ends instead of the bar directly. A towel is thicker and softer than a bar. The hold is harder. Your fingers must squeeze the towel to prevent slipping.</p>
        <p>Towel hangs make the support grip harder because the fingers cannot lock around a rigid bar.</p>
        <p>Hold times are often shorter than on a bar. Start with 10 to 20 seconds for 3 sets. Use a gym towel folded once for medium difficulty. A thick bath towel is harder. Full protocols are in the <a href="/towel-hang/">towel dead hang guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>One-Arm Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>Grab the bar with one hand and let your opposite arm hang at your side. Your entire bodyweight passes through one forearm, one shoulder and one side of your trunk. The load on each arm doubles compared with a two-arm hang.</p>
        <p>This is an advanced variation. Before one-arm progressions, aim for a controlled two-arm hang of about 60 seconds. This is a conservative guide and not a tested threshold. Insufficient preparation risks forearm, elbow and shoulder irritation.</p>
        <p>Start with assisted one-arm hangs. Wrap 2 to 3 fingers of your free hand around your hanging wrist for partial support. Hold 5 to 10 seconds per arm for 2 to 3 sets. Progress to unsupported holds gradually. The <a href="/one-arm-deadhang/">one-arm dead hang guide</a> covers the full progression.</p>

        <h2>Weighted Dead Hang</h2>
        <p>Attach external weight using a dip belt, weight vest or dumbbell between the feet. Add load only after a comfortable and pain-free bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds. Weighted hangs make the hold harder without a longer time on the bar.</p>
        <p>Progressive overload through added weight follows the same principle as barbell training. Your forearm muscles adapt to higher loads.</p>
        <p>Hold 15 to 30 seconds for 3 to 4 sets with 90 to 120 seconds rest. Keep at least one rest day between hard sessions. There are no published standards for added load. Add weight in small steps and record what you used. Full loading protocols are in the <a href="/weighted-hang-guide/">weighted dead hang guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>L-Hang</h2>
        <p>Hang from the bar and raise your legs to 90 degrees so your body forms an L shape. This adds core demand to the standard dead hang. Your abdominals, hip flexors and quads hold the leg position.</p>
        <p>The L-hang combines grip and core training. Your forearms still support your bodyweight while your abdominals hold the legs up. Hold time is shorter than a standard hang because your abs also tire.</p>
        <p>Start with bent-knee L-hangs if straight legs are too hard. Hold for 10 to 30 seconds across 3 sets. Move to straight legs once you can hold bent-knee for 30 seconds. Rest 90 seconds between sets.</p>

        <h2>Choosing the Right Variation</h2>
        <p>Your training goal decides which variation to prioritize. Use this table to match your goal with a dead hang type.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Goal</th><th scope="col">Variation</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Grip strength</td><td>Overhand, towel, weighted</td><td>Harder holds for the finger flexors</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Stretching after sitting</td><td>Passive hang</td><td>Shoulders and trunk hang relaxed</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Shoulder discomfort</td><td>Ask a clinician first</td><td>Evidence for hanging in shoulder rehab is limited (see the shoulder health guide)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Pull-up prep</td><td>Active hang</td><td>Practices the shoulder blade position used at the start of a pull-up</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Core training</td><td>L-hang and mixed grip</td><td>Trunk steadiness and hip flexor demand</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Climbing</td><td>Towel, one-arm</td><td>Thicker grip surface and single-arm work</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
        </div>

        <p>Many training programs combine 2 to 3 variations across a week. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days and add a session only if you stay pain free. The <a href="/training-programs/">structured dead hang programs</a> set out weekly schedules, and the <a href="/deadhang-guide/#workout-plan">dead hang workout plan</a> covers a single session.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="faq"><h3>What are the different types of dead hangs?</h3><p>The ten dead hang variations on this site are passive, active, overhand, underhand, neutral grip, mixed grip, towel, one-arm, weighted and L-hang. They change grip, shoulder position or load. The standard overhand passive hang is the starting point.</p></div>
        <div class="faq"><h3>How do I make a dead hang harder?</h3><p>Common ways to make a dead hang harder are a towel hang, a weighted hang, a one-arm hang and an L-hang. Each makes the hold harder in a different way. Add weight only after a comfortable, pain-free bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds.</p></div>
        <div class="faq"><h3>Is an active or passive dead hang better?</h3><p>Neither is better for every goal. A passive hang suits stretching after sitting. An active hang practices the shoulder blade position used at the start of a pull-up and is usually harder to hold. Many programs alternate them across sessions.</p></div>
        <div class="faq"><h3>Which dead hang variation is best for grip strength?</h3><p>The overhand, towel and weighted hangs make the hold harder for the finger flexors, so they suit grip strength training. Keep double overhand as the main hang and add one or two other variations across the week.</p></div>
        <div class="faq"><h3>Is the one-arm dead hang safe to try?</h3><p>The one-arm dead hang is an advanced variation. Start after a controlled two-arm hang of about 60 seconds, which is a conservative guide and not a tested threshold. Begin with assisted holds and stop if your forearm, elbow or shoulder hurts.</p></div>
        
